WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.080
In the first film, you met a glowing reporter and a light-gated actuator.

2
00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:12.980
In a brain with billions of cells, how do you put them into one chosen cell type?

3
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:19.160
Almost every cell holds the same chromosomes, so control comes from where a gene is

4
00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:20.160
read.

5
00:00:20.483 --> 00:00:25.803
Start with a transgene, a piece of engineered DNA added to a fertilized egg.

6
00:00:27.083 --> 00:00:28.863
Every transgene pairs two parts.

7
00:00:30.103 --> 00:00:32.623
The promoter decides where and when a gene runs.

8
00:00:33.903 --> 00:00:37.023
The coding sequence decides which protein the cell builds.

9
00:00:38.067 --> 00:00:42.967
Pair a promoter active in every cell with the green fluorescent protein gene.

10
00:00:44.227 --> 00:00:47.147
Every cell reads it, and you get a glowing green mouse.

11
00:00:48.407 --> 00:00:52.147
That proves a transgene works, yet every neuron lights up at once.

12
00:00:53.327 --> 00:00:55.447
You still cannot isolate one cell type.

13
00:00:56.450 --> 00:01:00.550
To target one cell type cleanly, split the job across two genes.

14
00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:06.370
Put your reporter behind a strong promoter, then block it with a stop signal that

15
00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:12.370
halts reading. Flank that stop block with two short tags called loxP, pointing the

16
00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:16.810
same way. With the stop intact, every cell stays dark.

17
00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:22.833
Now bring in a recombinase called Cre, an enzyme from a bacterial virus.

18
00:01:24.153 --> 00:01:28.653
A recombinase cuts and rejoins DNA, and Cre does it at loxP sites.

19
00:01:30.053 --> 00:01:31.053
Here is the twist.

20
00:01:32.373 --> 00:01:34.273
Cre turns a gene on by deleting DNA.

21
00:01:35.693 --> 00:01:40.313
It cuts out the stop block, clears the roadblock, and lets the cell build the tool.

22
00:01:41.383 --> 00:01:42.803
So which cells make Cre?

23
00:01:44.043 --> 00:01:49.883
Place the Cre gene behind a promoter active mainly in neuron type A. Type A makes

24
00:01:49.883 --> 00:01:52.363
Cre, snips out the stop, and lights up green.

25
00:01:53.883 --> 00:01:57.623
Neighboring cells never make Cre, keep their stop blocks, and stay dark.

26
00:01:58.667 --> 00:02:00.507
Pause here for a key consequence.

27
00:02:01.967 --> 00:02:05.567
Cre removed that stop block from neuron A's chromosome for good.

28
00:02:06.827 --> 00:02:10.987
Suppose that cell later divides, and its daughter cells stop making Cre.

29
00:02:12.287 --> 00:02:15.107
Do those daughter cells still make the glowing reporter?

30
00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:27.550
Yes, they do. Because the stop block is gone from the chromosome, every descendant

31
00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:29.250
inherits the unlocked gene.

32
00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:33.770
That permanent mark lets biologists trace cell lineages.

33
00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:36.210
Yet it carries a catch.

34
00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:43.150
Brief early Cre activity can unlock unintended cells too, so check every Cre line

35
00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:44.150
with a reporter.

36
00:02:44.933 --> 00:02:47.273
Need a second tool in neuron type B?

37
00:02:48.373 --> 00:02:51.653
Add Flp, a yeast enzyme that cuts only its own sites.

38
00:02:52.893 --> 00:02:57.853
Drive Cre in type A and Flp in type B, and each unlocks its own tool cleanly.

39
00:02:59.113 --> 00:03:02.673
Promoters and recombinases place each tool in chosen cells.
